WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.159
Film Courage: How important is it for a writer
to know their own personal story?

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:06.020
Not simply the story they’re writing but
who they are?

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.590
Their own life journey?

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:13.599
Karl Iglesias: So if you’re talking for
a writer, I would think it’s not that important?

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:14.599
Film Courage: No?

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:15.599
Karl Iglesias: No.

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:19.290
I know it might be surprising to hear, but
to me it’s not that important?

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.470
Film Courage: Why not?

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:25.170
Karl Iglesias: The thing you [as a writer]
should be aware of is human dynamics.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:30.610
You know, psychology, emotions, character
relationships.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.910
That you should be well-versed in.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:40.440
Now, lucky is the writer that has had an amazing
life experience and has had all those experiences

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.570
that he can write about.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.210
That makes it easier.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.989
But it’s not necessary, if you’re a highly
curious person.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:52.309
I mean you could be a great writer that has
had a very mediocre life.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:58.450
I’m not a believer of the Hemingway thing,
you know, where if you want to write about

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.150
adventure, you’ve got to go and be adventurous.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:07.390
Maybe there is some truth to it but I think
it really depends on your mind and your curiosity.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.990
And I think we can all find…it’s all in
your imagination.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:15.280
If you’re highly imaginative you can write
about anything, whether you experience it

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.610
or not.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:22.700
If you write science fiction, George Lucas
didn’t experience going on a ship in order

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.470
to be able to write about it, right?

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.420
Or James Cameron?

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.970
Do you think all that stuff about Pandora
and AVATAR that he experienced that in his

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:31.970
own life…no!

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.520
It’s all imagination, right?

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.549
So that’s what you need to know.

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:39.710
You need to have high imagination, obviously.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.990
But then you need to be well-versed in character
relationships.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.080
So yes.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.770
If you had experience with character relationships,
that’s great (character conflicts).

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.670
But if you haven’t, you can always learn
it.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.990
And if you’re highly-curious…I mean I
have a psychology degree, so if I wasn’t

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.540
a writer, I’d be a psychologist.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:58.960
Because that is what fascinates me.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.040
I’m fascinated my human interactions, psychology
and especially the reason why people do what

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.700
they do, which is what characters do in stories.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.310
So really, that works for me.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:14.260
But my life story, as much as I consider it
very unique and different from most people’s

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.260
life stories, I don’t have the desire to
write about it.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.430
So it doesn’t affect me at all in that sense.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:25.840
Film Courage: So someone shouldn’t totally…well…let
me just rephrase that.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.900
Karl Iglesias: I think I understand what you’re
saying.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.621
Maybe it might be different for actors because
that is usually what you’re taught, you

00:02:33.621 --> 00:02:37.150
have to use your sense memory and that kind
of thing.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.180
But for writers, I don’t think there is
a Yes or a No.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.180
For me, I don’t think it’s important.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.239
It doesn’t have to be.

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:45.239
For others it might be.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:50.209
And I’m aware that if it did happen to you
and you want to write about it, then of course

00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:53.040
that makes it easier as it happens, you’ll
me a lot more realistic.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:03:02.300
Film Courage: Well you talked about today
that when you gave your class, many people

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.980
noted that they wanted to write their own
story.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.860
Karl Iglesias: A lot of people are writing
that.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.670
That’s because they think that their life
stories are interesting.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.620
Film Courage: Everyone thinks their life story
is interesting.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.620
Karl Iglesias: Right.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.090
Everyone thinks they’re the hero of their
own story and there is a little truth to it.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:23.849
But I have to say if I’m talking from experience
from hearing people’s stories that they

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:30.310
want to write as movies, I would say that
90 percent of them are not that interesting.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.400
The writers themselves think it’s interesting.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.000
I don’t think it’s interesting and audiences
don’t think it’s interesting.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.560
So they’ve got to learn that.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.980
That’s where feedback comes in.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.640
If the feedback is “Okay, well you know?”

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.171
I mean I always bring out Hitchcock’s quote
about “Drama is real life without all the

00:03:47.171 --> 00:03:49.570
boring parts cut out.”

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:55.819
So a lot of people feel that just because
they’ve had a few interesting experiences

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:59.920
(maybe unique) that it warrants them to making
a movie.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.970
They may be right, but a lot of times they
are not.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.159
Film Courage: I’ve found that some people
who have had incredibly interesting (albeit

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:10.799
hard lives) actually don’t think they are
that interesting.

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:12.739
And that the reverse is actually the case.

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:15.360
The people who have had “Oh yes and this
person said this and this happened.”

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.139
etc. and that [easy or charmed life story]
is not actually that exciting.

00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:21.989
But I’ve talked to several people and their
lives have been very difficult and challenging

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:25.069
and they don’t really see it as anything
interesting.

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:26.810
And I’m always encouraging them [to write
about their lives].

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:28.009
Karl Iglesias: That’s where feedback comes
in!

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:29.169
Film Courage: And these people are like “No.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:30.169
This is boring.”

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.460
Karl Iglesias: Because if they hear everybody
telling them, eventually they will believe

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:33.460
it.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.460
So…get feedback.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.460
Film Courage: Interesting…

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.330
Question for the Viewers: What are your thoughts
on a writer knowing their personal story?

